12. PHASE TEN: BUILD CLAIM-LEVEL DESCRIPTION VALIDATION

The rewritten description must be checked independently before it can be approved.

Do not assume that a fluent or professional description is accurate.

Create or improve a validation process that compares the proposed description against the source claims and verified property fields.

For each material claim, determine whether it is:

* Preserved accurately.
* Faithfully paraphrased.
* Missing from the proposed description.
* Contradicted.
* Unsupported.
* Ambiguous.
* Prohibited by publishing rules.
* In need of human review.

The validator must detect omissions as well as invented claims.

For example, if the source contains six material property features and the rewritten description mentions only three, the system must identify the omitted features.

Do not rely exclusively on keyword matching.

Use deterministic checks for exact structured values and contextual comparison for semantic differences.

If AI is used to evaluate semantic equivalence, it may only be invoked within an explicitly user-initiated editing or publishing validation workflow. It must not become a hidden automatic import operation.

A description must not pass merely because its overall quality score is high.

Critical factual contradictions and unsupported material claims must block approval until resolved.

