13. PHASE ELEVEN: IMPLEMENT SAFE CONTENT SANITIZATION

Inspect all current publishing rules, sanitizers, prompts, and post-processing functions.

Determine which content is prohibited, which information is required, and which source details must be retained.

Use deterministic rules for clearly identifiable prohibited content and contextual review for ambiguous cases.

Sanitization must not delete an entire sentence simply because one part is prohibited if the legitimate property facts can be preserved accurately.

For example, removing an external promotional link must not also remove a valid statement about the property’s fenced yard.

If a prohibited statement contains a legitimate material fact, preserve the fact separately when permitted and appropriate.

Apply the same versioned publishing policy across:

* Manual editing.
* AI-assisted rewriting.
* Bulk publishing preparation.
* Administrative publishing.
* Other supported publishing routes.

After sanitization, validate the final description again.

Do not silently remove essential facts to satisfy a formatting or policy rule.

